      "s65-c2-income",
      "The Income Statement",
      "The income statement shows a company's sales, costs, and profit over a period of time. It starts with revenue and subtracts costs until it reaches net income, or net profit.\n1. Revenue minus the cost of goods sold (COGS), such as materials and labor, gives gross income.\n2. Gross income minus operating expenses gives operating income. Operating expenses include selling, general, and administrative (SG&A) costs, like sales salaries and marketing, and research and development.\n3. Depreciation and amortization are also operating expenses. They are noncash expenses. Depreciation is the loss in value of physical things like buildings and machines. Amortization is the loss in value of things you cannot touch, like patents and brand names.\n4. Subtract interest on debt to get earnings before taxes (EBT).\n5. Subtract taxes to get net income. Net income divided by sales is the profit margin.\n\nStraight-line depreciation spreads the loss evenly: (cost − salvage value) ÷ expected life. A $1 million machine with a 10-year life and a $100,000 salvage value is depreciated $90,000 a year. Accelerated depreciation counts more of the cost in the early years, because assets lose more value when new.\n\nTwo key formulas:\n• Earnings per share (EPS) = (net income − preferred dividends) ÷ common shares outstanding. It shows profit per share.\n• Price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio = market price ÷ EPS. It shows what investors pay for each dollar of earnings. A high P/E means investors expect strong future profits. High-P/E stocks are usually growth stocks and tend to be riskier. Low-P/E stocks are often value stocks.",
