      "s65-c2-balance",
      "The Balance Sheet",
      "The balance sheet lists what a company owns (assets), what it owes (liabilities), and what is left for owners (shareholders' equity, also called net worth or book value). Unlike the other two statements, it shows one moment in time, like a snapshot.\n\nassets − liabilities = net worth, so assets = liabilities + net worth. The two sides always balance.\n\nAssets are listed by liquidity. Current assets, which turn into cash within a year, come first. Then come long-term assets. Liabilities are listed the same way. Current liabilities, due within a year, like accounts payable and wages payable, come first. Long-term liabilities, like bonds, come next.\n\nInventory can be valued two ways:\n• FIFO (first in, first out): the oldest items are counted as sold first. When prices are rising, cheaper old items are sold first, so reported earnings are higher.\n• LIFO (last in, first out): the newest items are counted as sold first. When prices are rising, pricier new items are sold first, so taxable income is lower. The older items left behind make inventory look less valuable.\n\nRatios that show if a company can pay its bills:\n• Working capital = current assets − current liabilities. A positive number means it can likely pay short-term bills. It is also called net current assets.\n• Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities. Below 1.0 may be a danger sign.\n• Quick ratio is like the current ratio but leaves out inventory and prepaid expenses. It shows whether a company can stay afloat without selling inventory.\n• Debt-to-equity ratio = total debt ÷ shareholders' equity. A high number means heavy leverage, which may be a bad sign.\n\nThe cash flow statement shows how much cash a company makes, where it comes from, and where it goes. It covers a period of time. It leaves out noncash items like depreciation but includes capital spending and changes in inventory, receivables, and payables.",
