      "s65-c2-technical",
      "Reading Price Charts",
      "A technical analyst studies price trends and trades on them. They ignore financial statements.\n\nTechnical analysis rests on two beliefs. First, all known information is already in a security's price, so hunting for bargains does not pay. Second, prices move in trends and patterns that can predict future prices.\n\nSupport and resistance. When a price rises, it may hit a ceiling called resistance. The analyst thinks the stock is overbought, meaning too many people bought it and the price should soon drop. When a price falls, it may hit a floor called support. The stock is thought to be oversold and should soon rise. The range between support and resistance is a trading channel. A stock that stays in its channel a long time has consolidated.\n\nMoving averages. A moving average is the average price over a set period, such as the last 200 days, updated each day. When a short-term average (like 20 days) rises above a long-term average (like 200 days), analysts see a bullish sign. When it drops below, they expect prices to fall further.\n\nShort-interest ratio = short positions ÷ average daily trading volume. Analysts think heavy short selling means the price will rise later, because short sellers must buy shares back to close their positions.\n\nOdd lot theory. An odd lot is fewer than 100 shares. The theory says small investors trade odd lots and are always late to trends. So when odd-lot investors buy, the analyst sells, and when they sell, the analyst buys.",
